<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<paragraphs xml:id="para-functional" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Functional</title>
  <idx>functional</idx>

  <p>
    A <term>functional</term> is a mapping <m>J : \mathcal{F} \to \mathbb{R}</m>, where <m>\mathcal{F}</m> denotes a
    function space.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-functional">
    <title>Standard Form of a Functional</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
          In the calculus of variations, the <alert>standard form</alert> of a functional <m>J[y]</m> is typically written as
            <md>
              J[y] = \int_{x_1}^{x_2}{F\big(x,y(x),y_x(x)\big)\,dx} \, .
            </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>



  <remark>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A point along an <xref ref="def-admissible-path" text="custom">admissible path</xref> that gives a stationary
        functional is not necessarily a minimum or maximum. It may also be a saddle point.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The requirement that a function must be stationary gives rise to the <xref ref="def-euler-lagrange" text="custom">
      Euler-Lagrange equation</xref>.
    </p>
  </remark>

</paragraphs>